      "eyebrow": "How to read this",
      "heading": "Typical frequencies, not fixed legal minimums",
      "text": "Most recurring UK compliance checks are governed by a duty to keep something safe, not by a single legal number. The regulator sets the outcome and expects a documented risk assessment to set the interval. Where a fixed period does exist in law, such as the six-monthly thorough examination of a hoist under LOLER or the annual MOT, we state it plainly. Everywhere else we say typical or commonly, name the body that requires the record, and note that your own risk assessment and current guidance decide the real interval. If in doubt, check the regulator's live guidance before you set a schedule."
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "eyebrow": "Sector 1 · Care homes",
      "heading": "Care homes (CQC England, Care Inspectorate Scotland, CIW Wales, RQIA Northern Ireland)",
      "text": "Care providers carry duties under the Health and Social Care Act, the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005, the Health and Safety at Work Act, LOLER, COSHH and the Control of Legionella guidance (HSG274 / ACoP L8). The frequencies below are common practice. Your fire risk assessment, water risk assessment and equipment schedules set the real intervals."
    },
    {
      "type": "cards",
      "eyebrow": "Care homes",
      "heading": "Typical recurring checks",
      "items": [
        { "kicker": "Weekly", "title": "Fire alarm test", "text": "A different call point tested each week is common practice under BS 5839. The Fire Safety Order requires the system to be maintained; the weekly test is the widely used method." },
        { "kicker": "Six-monthly", "title": "LOLER hoist examination", "text": "Thorough examination of hoists and slings that lift people is required at least every 6 months under LOLER 1998. This one is a fixed legal minimum." },
        { "kicker": "Risk-assessed", "title": "Legionella / water temperature", "text": "Monthly hot and cold outlet temperature checks are common under HSG274, but the exact monitoring regime is set by your written water risk assessment." },
        { "kicker": "Annual", "title": "PAT (portable appliances)", "text": "Portable appliance testing has no fixed legal interval. Annual is common for care settings; the interval should be risk-assessed per appliance type and environment." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "eyebrow": "Sector 2 · Schools and education estates",
      "heading": "Schools and education estates (DfE, HSE, local authority, academy trust)",
      "text": "Schools and colleges carry the same fire, legionella, asbestos and electrical duties as any employer, plus a duty of care to pupils. The DfE good-estate-management guidance and HSE cover most of it. Playground and PE equipment adds an annual competent-person inspection on top of routine visual checks."
    },
    {
      "type": "cards",
      "eyebrow": "Schools",
      "heading": "Typical recurring checks",
      "items": [
        { "kicker": "Termly", "title": "Fire evacuation drill", "text": "A fire drill each term (three a year) is standard practice for UK schools so every pupil and staff pattern is covered. The Fire Safety Order requires drills; termly is the common cadence." },
        { "kicker": "Annual + review", "title": "Fire risk assessment review", "text": "The fire risk assessment must be kept up to date and reviewed regularly, commonly at least annually and after any material change to the building or use." },
        { "kicker": "Annual + routine", "title": "Playground / PE equipment", "text": "An annual inspection by a competent person is standard (RPII for playgrounds), backed by routine visual and operational checks through the year." },
        { "kicker": "Ongoing", "title": "Asbestos management", "text": "Where an asbestos management plan exists, the condition of known ACMs is re-inspected at intervals set by the plan, commonly at least annually, under the Control of Asbestos Regulations 2012." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "eyebrow": "Sector 3 · Fleet and transport",
      "heading": "Fleet and transport (DVSA, DfT)",
      "text": "Operators of goods vehicles and PSVs work to the DVSA Guide to Maintaining Roadworthiness. Two items here are firm. The daily walkaround check before first use is required, and the annual MOT (or first use anniversary test for HGVs and PSVs) is a fixed legal test. Safety inspection intervals are set by the operator based on vehicle type and use, and stated on the operator licence."
    },
    {
      "type": "cards",
      "eyebrow": "Fleet",
      "heading": "Typical recurring checks",
      "items": [
        { "kicker": "Daily", "title": "Driver walkaround check", "text": "A documented walkaround / first-use check before the vehicle is driven is required for commercial vehicles. Defects must be reported and recorded. This is a firm DVSA expectation." },
        { "kicker": "Operator-set", "title": "Safety inspection", "text": "Periodic safety inspections at a set interval (commonly every 4 to 13 weeks depending on vehicle age, type and mileage) declared on the operator licence. The interval is chosen by risk, not fixed nationally." },
        { "kicker": "Annual", "title": "MOT / annual test", "text": "Cars and vans from 3 years old, and HGVs / PSVs annually, must pass the statutory test. This is a fixed legal requirement." },
        { "kicker": "As used", "title": "Tachograph", "text": "Drivers download and check tachograph data on a rolling basis (driver card commonly every 28 days, vehicle unit at least every 90 days) under EU / GB drivers hours rules; calibration every 2 years." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "eyebrow": "Sector 4 · Food businesses",
      "heading": "Food businesses (FSA, local authority EHO, HACCP)",
      "text": "Food businesses must have a food safety management system based on HACCP principles, commonly the FSA Safer Food, Better Business pack for smaller operators. The local authority environmental health officer inspects and rates the business. Most day-to-day frequencies are set inside the business own HACCP plan rather than by a single national number."
    },
    {
      "type": "cards",
      "eyebrow": "Food",
      "heading": "Typical recurring checks",
      "items": [
        { "kicker": "Daily", "title": "Temperature logs", "text": "Fridge, freezer and hot-holding temperatures are commonly checked and recorded at least daily, and cooking / cooling temperatures at each service, as set by the HACCP plan." },
        { "kicker": "Daily + deep", "title": "Cleaning schedule", "text": "A documented cleaning schedule runs daily for food-contact surfaces, with periodic deep cleans. Frequencies are set by the business own plan and risk." },
        { "kicker": "On change", "title": "HACCP review", "text": "The food safety management system is reviewed whenever the menu, process, supplier or premises change, and commonly at least annually as good practice." },
        { "kicker": "Ongoing", "title": "Allergen and pest control", "text": "Allergen information kept accurate for every dish (Natasha law for prepacked-for-direct-sale). Pest control monitored continuously, with contractor visits at a risk-assessed interval." }
      ]
    },
