  "articlePanels": [{"kind":"lede","html":"<p>This is a plain-English reference for how often each recurring UK compliance check is <strong>typically</strong> required, by sector, with the regulator named. It is written to be quoted. Where a frequency is fixed in law we say so. Where it is set by risk assessment, which is most of them, we say that instead of inventing a number.</p><p>Important: many of these intervals are set by your own written risk assessment, not by a single legal figure. Always confirm against the current guidance from the named regulator before you set a schedule.</p>"},{"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>Most UK compliance frequencies are risk-assessed, not fixed by law. The regulator sets the outcome; your risk assessment sets the interval.</li><li>A few are genuinely fixed: LOLER thorough examination of a hoist is at least every 6 months; the MOT / annual vehicle test is annual; the DVSA driver walkaround is before first use each day.</li><li>Fire alarm test weekly, emergency lighting monthly plus annual, fire extinguisher service annual are long-standing common practice under the relevant British Standards.</li><li>PAT has no fixed legal interval. Annual is common in higher-risk settings, but it should be risk-assessed.</li><li>Food and care frequencies mostly live inside your HACCP plan or care risk assessments, not in a national number.</li><li>This page is a starting point. Confirm every item against the named regulator's live guidance.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Sector 1","heading":"Care homes","html":"<p>Regulated by the CQC in England, the Care Inspectorate in Scotland, CIW in Wales and RQIA in Northern Ireland, on top of HSE, fire and LOLER duties.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fire alarm test</td><td>Weekly (rotating call point)</td><td>RRO 2005 / BS 5839</td></tr><tr><td>Fire drill</td><td>Commonly every 6 months, more for night staff</td><td>RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>Fire door checks</td><td>Commonly every 6 months (quarterly in higher-risk)</td><td>RRO 2005 / Fire Safety (England) Regs</td></tr><tr><td>Legionella / water temperatures</td><td>Commonly monthly outlet temps; regime set by water risk assessment</td><td>HSE HSG274 / ACoP L8</td></tr><tr><td>LOLER hoist thorough examination</td><td>At least every 6 months (fixed)</td><td>LOLER 1998</td></tr><tr><td>PAT</td><td>Commonly annual; risk-assessed, no fixed interval</td><td>Electricity at Work Regs 1989</td></tr><tr><td>IPC (infection prevention) audit</td><td>Commonly monthly to quarterly</td><td>CQC / Care Inspectorate expectation</td></tr><tr><td>Medication (MAR) audit</td><td>Commonly monthly</td><td>CQC / NICE good practice</td></tr><tr><td>Health and safety walk</td><td>Commonly monthly</td><td>HSWA 1974 duty</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Care frequencies for IPC, medication and H&S walks are provider policy set to meet the regulator's expectations, not a single legal number. Confirm against your regulator's current guidance.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Sector 2","heading":"Schools and education estates","html":"<p>Schools and colleges follow DfE good estate management guidance, HSE duties and the same fire, legionella and asbestos law as any employer.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fire evacuation drill</td><td>Termly (three a year)</td><td>RRO 2005 / DfE practice</td></tr><tr><td>Fire risk assessment review</td><td>Commonly at least annually, and on material change</td><td>RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>Legionella monitoring</td><td>Commonly monthly temps; regime by water risk assessment</td><td>HSE HSG274 / ACoP L8</td></tr><tr><td>Asbestos management check</td><td>Re-inspection of known ACMs at plan interval, commonly at least annual</td><td>Control of Asbestos Regs 2012</td></tr><tr><td>PAT</td><td>Commonly annual; risk-assessed</td><td>Electricity at Work Regs 1989</td></tr><tr><td>Playground / PE equipment</td><td>Annual competent-person inspection plus routine visual checks</td><td>RPII / BS EN 1176 practice</td></tr><tr><td>Emergency lighting</td><td>Monthly function test plus annual full test</td><td>BS 5266 / RRO 2005</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Termly fire drills and annual playground inspections are established practice rather than a single stated legal number. Confirm with your local authority or trust policy.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Sector 3","heading":"Fleet and transport","html":"<p>Operators follow the DVSA Guide to Maintaining Roadworthiness. Walkaround and MOT are firm; safety-inspection intervals are operator-set on the licence.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Driver walkaround check</td><td>Daily, before first use (fixed expectation)</td><td>DVSA</td></tr><tr><td>Safety inspection</td><td>Operator-set interval, commonly every 4 to 13 weeks</td><td>DVSA / operator licence</td></tr><tr><td>MOT / annual test</td><td>Annual (fixed)</td><td>DVSA</td></tr><tr><td>Tachograph download</td><td>Driver card commonly every 28 days; vehicle unit at least every 90 days</td><td>EU / GB drivers hours rules</td></tr><tr><td>Tachograph calibration</td><td>Every 2 years</td><td>DVSA / approved centre</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The safety-inspection frequency is chosen by the operator from vehicle age, type and use, then declared on the operator licence. It is not a single national figure.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Sector 4","heading":"Food businesses","html":"<p>Food businesses run a HACCP-based food safety management system (often the FSA Safer Food, Better Business pack) and are inspected and rated by the local authority EHO.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Temperature logs</td><td>At least daily; cooking / cooling at each service</td><td>FSA / HACCP plan</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning schedule</td><td>Daily, with periodic deep cleans</td><td>FSA / HACCP plan</td></tr><tr><td>HACCP review</td><td>On any change; commonly at least annual</td><td>Regulation (EC) 852/2004</td></tr><tr><td>Allergen check</td><td>Ongoing; accurate for every dish</td><td>FSA / Natasha law</td></tr><tr><td>Pest control</td><td>Continuous monitoring; contractor visits at risk-assessed interval</td><td>FSA / HACCP plan</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Most food frequencies are set inside your own HACCP plan rather than by a fixed national number. The EHO checks that your plan exists, is followed and is recorded.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Sector 5","heading":"General workplace","html":"<p>Baseline HSE, fire and electrical duties that apply to every UK employer, on top of any sector list above.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fire extinguisher service</td><td>Annual basic service plus monthly visual check</td><td>BS 5306 / RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>Emergency lighting</td><td>Monthly function test plus annual full-duration test</td><td>BS 5266 / RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>Fire alarm test</td><td>Weekly (rotating call point)</td><td>BS 5839 / RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>PAT</td><td>Risk-assessed; no fixed legal interval</td><td>Electricity at Work Regs 1989</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed wiring (EICR)</td><td>Commonly up to every 5 years for many workplaces</td><td>Electricity at Work Regs 1989 / BS 7671</td></tr><tr><td>First aid provision</td><td>Needs assessed; kit commonly checked monthly</td><td>Health and Safety (First-Aid) Regs 1981</td></tr><tr><td>RIDDOR reporting</td><td>Not a schedule; report reportable events when they occur</td><td>RIDDOR 2013 / HSE</td></tr></tbody></table><p>RIDDOR is a reporting duty, not a recurring check. Specified injuries, occupational diseases and dangerous occurrences must be reported to the HSE within the RIDDOR time limits.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The tie-in, kept short","heading":"Capturing the record on the day","html":"<p>Knowing the frequency is half the job. The other half is having a dated, defensible record that the check actually happened, done at the point of work and not written up later from memory.</p><p>Quickler lets a firm schedule any recurring check above as a WhatsApp workflow. On the due day, the person on the job gets the questions in their existing WhatsApp chat and answers them, adding a photo where it helps. The result is a dated, photo-backed record and a shareable web report, with no app to install and no login. Pricing is [[price:bundles]], with a free tier of 20 reports a month. See the <a href=\"/pages/demo.html\">demo</a>.</p>"},{"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>How often must a care home test its fire alarm?</h3><p>Weekly is standard practice. A different manual call point is tested each week so that, over time, every point is checked, following BS 5839. The Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 requires the alarm system to be maintained in working order; the weekly test is the common way to meet that. Your fire risk assessment may require more.</p><h3>How often is a LOLER inspection required on a hoist?</h3><p>At least every 6 months. Under the Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment Regulations 1998, equipment used to lift people, such as a care-home hoist and its slings, must have a thorough examination by a competent person at least every six months. This is a fixed legal minimum, not a risk-assessed one.</p><h3>How often must a school do a fire drill?</h3><p>Termly is the common cadence, giving three drills a year, so that different times of day and staff patterns are covered. The Fire Safety Order requires that staff and occupants can evacuate safely and that this is practised; termly is established school practice rather than a single stated legal number.</p><h3>How often does a commercial vehicle need a walkaround check?</h3><p>Every day, before the vehicle is first used. The DVSA expects a documented daily walkaround check of a goods vehicle or PSV, with any defects reported and recorded before the vehicle goes out. This is a firm requirement, separate from the annual MOT and the operator-set safety inspection.</p><h3>Is PAT testing a legal requirement every year?</h3><p>No. Portable appliance testing has no fixed legal interval. The Electricity at Work Regulations 1989 require electrical equipment to be maintained to prevent danger; the frequency should be risk-assessed by appliance type and environment. Annual is common in higher-risk settings such as care homes, but it is not a legal minimum.</p><h3>Are these frequencies legally fixed?</h3><p>Mostly not. A few are fixed, such as the six-monthly LOLER hoist examination and the annual MOT. The majority are set by your own written risk assessment to meet a duty set by the regulator. Always confirm each item against the current guidance from the named body before you set a schedule.</p>"}],
