  "seoBody": "<h2>How often must each UK compliance check be done?</h2><p>This is a plain-English reference for how often each recurring UK compliance check is typically required, by sector, with the regulator named. Most UK compliance frequencies are set by a written risk assessment rather than by a single legal number. The regulator sets the outcome to be achieved, and expects a documented risk assessment to set the interval. Where a fixed period does exist in law, such as the six-monthly LOLER thorough examination of a hoist or the annual MOT, we state it plainly. Everywhere else we say typical or commonly and name the body that requires the record.</p><h2>Care homes (CQC, Care Inspectorate, CIW, RQIA)</h2><p>Fire alarm test: weekly rotating call point (BS 5839). Fire drill: commonly every six months. Fire door checks: commonly six-monthly. Legionella and water temperatures: commonly monthly outlet temperatures, with the regime set by the water risk assessment (HSE HSG274). LOLER hoist thorough examination: at least every six months, a fixed legal minimum. PAT: commonly annual but risk-assessed. IPC audit: commonly monthly to quarterly. Medication (MAR) audit: commonly monthly. Health and safety walk: commonly monthly. IPC, medication and H&S walk frequencies are provider policy set to meet the regulator's expectations.</p><h2>Schools and education estates (DfE, HSE)</h2><p>Fire evacuation drill: termly, three a year. Fire risk assessment review: commonly at least annually and on any material change. Legionella monitoring: commonly monthly temperatures. Asbestos management check: re-inspection of known asbestos-containing materials at the plan interval, commonly at least annual (Control of Asbestos Regulations 2012). PAT: commonly annual, risk-assessed. Playground and PE equipment: annual competent-person inspection plus routine visual checks. Emergency lighting: monthly function test plus annual full test (BS 5266).</p><h2>Fleet and transport (DVSA)</h2><p>Driver walkaround check: daily, before first use, a firm DVSA expectation. Safety inspection: operator-set interval, commonly every four to thirteen weeks, declared on the operator licence. MOT or annual test: annual, a fixed legal requirement. Tachograph download: driver card commonly every 28 days, vehicle unit at least every 90 days. Tachograph calibration: every two years.</p><h2>Food businesses (FSA, EHO, HACCP)</h2><p>Temperature logs: at least daily, with cooking and cooling checks at each service. Cleaning schedule: daily with periodic deep cleans. HACCP review: on any change and commonly at least annual. Allergen check: ongoing and accurate for every dish. Pest control: continuous monitoring with contractor visits at a risk-assessed interval. Most food frequencies live inside the business own HACCP plan rather than in a national number.</p><h2>General workplace (HSE)</h2><p>Fire extinguisher service: annual basic service plus monthly visual check (BS 5306). Emergency lighting: monthly function test plus annual full-duration test (BS 5266). PAT: risk-assessed, no fixed legal interval. Fixed wiring (EICR): commonly up to every five years for many workplaces. First aid: needs assessed, kit commonly checked monthly. RIDDOR: not a schedule but a duty to report specified injuries, diseases and dangerous occurrences to the HSE when they occur.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How often must a care home test its fire alarm?</h3><p>Weekly is standard practice, testing a different call point each week under BS 5839. The Fire Safety Order requires the system to be maintained; the weekly test is the common method.</p><h3>How often is a LOLER inspection required on a hoist?</h3><p>At least every six months. Under LOLER 1998, equipment used to lift people must have a thorough examination by a competent person at least every six months. This is a fixed legal minimum.</p><h3>How often must a school do a fire drill?</h3><p>Termly is the common cadence, giving three drills a year. It is established school practice rather than a single stated legal number.</p><h3>Is PAT testing a legal requirement every year?</h3><p>No. PAT has no fixed legal interval. The frequency should be risk-assessed. Annual is common in higher-risk settings but is not a legal minimum.</p><h2>Use this in Quickler</h2><p>Quickler lets a firm schedule any recurring check above as a WhatsApp workflow, so the person on the job records it on the due day, dated and photo-backed, with no app to install. Pricing is £20 per active user a month, dormant users free, with a free tier of 20 reports a month. See the <a href=\"/pages/demo.html\">demo</a>. This page is a reference and does not replace the current guidance of the named regulators.</p>",
